body:
`Aggregate<I>` addresses several collections as one range. There is no streaming counterpart. `Read::chain` is not one:

```text
a.chain(b)                  -> ["a1", "a2", "b1", "b2"]
a_without_newline.chain(b)  -> ["a1", "a2",       "b2"]
```

`b1` is gone and nothing reports it. `Reader` seeds a sentinel newline before byte 0 so a file opening with a delimiter needs no special case. That sentinel is per stream rather than per file. Chained, the bytes do hold `TTTT>b1 x` on one line, so three records is the right reading of them. This is not a `libsail` bug, which is what makes it dangerous: nothing is positioned to notice.

`sail` is already immune, because every multi-input command builds a fresh `Reader` per entry rather than chaining. That per-file loop is the behaviour to lift.

## Proposal

A new type in `index` that reads several paths as one run of records, with a fresh `Reader<File>` per path and one global index space:

```rust
impl Readers {
    pub fn new(paths: impl IntoIterator<Item = impl AsRef<Path>>, format: Format) -> Readers;
    pub fn count(&mut self) -> Result<usize>;
    pub fn advance(&mut self) -> Result<bool>;
    pub fn advance_to(&mut self, n: usize) -> Result<bool>;
    pub fn position(&self) -> Option<usize>;
    pub fn record(&self) -> &[u8];
}
```

```rust
let mut readers = Readers::new(paths, format);
let n = readers.count()?;

for at in Selection::new(n, m, seed) {
    if !readers.advance_to(at)? { break; }
    take(readers.record());
}
```

- It takes paths only (sail-a5d666), since `count` and the walk each open every path.
- `new` opens nothing, like `Reader::new`. A path that fails to open is an `Err` from the `advance`, `advance_to` or `count` that reaches it.
- `count` opens every path and keeps one count per path. It counts from the start of each file whatever the walk has reached.
- Once `count` has run, `advance_to(n)` skips every file that ends before record `n` without opening it. Before that, it frames every record it passes, file by file.
- `position`, `record` and the panic on going backwards follow `Reader`'s rules (sail-317db4).
- One format covers every path.

Nothing in the API names a part. A caller sees one run of records and a global position.

## Rejected

- `Reader::over` returning this type. A constructor on `Reader` that returns a different type reads as a `Reader`.
- `offset()` as a file index paired with that file's `Offset`. It puts the parts into the API.
- `offset()` as a byte offset into the files laid end to end. Every reader in `libsail` addresses one file, so using the value means mapping it back to a file, which needs the part boundaries again.
- A `FileIndex(usize)` newtype for the file index. It went with the file-index `offset()`.

## Out of scope

`offset()` waits on sail-9d931d, whose answer decides which kind of offset a multi-file index needs. `sail` gains no caller here, since `sail skim` takes one input.

## Cost

Additive. One new public type in `index`.
